@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.2. Packages @{ub}

Actually parsing an XML document is very simple: one line of code. However, before you get to that line of code, you need to take a short detour to talk about packages.

@{b}Example 9.5. Loading an XML document (a sneak peek) @{ub}

@{code}

>>> from xml.dom import minidom
>>> xmldoc = minidom.parse('~/diveintopython/common/py/kgp/binary.xml')
@{body}

This is a syntax you haven't seen before. It looks almost like the @{code}from @{i}module@{ui} import@{body} you know and love, but the @{code}"."@{body} gives it away as something above and beyond a simple import. In fact, @{code}xml@{body} is what is known as a package, @{code}dom@{body} is a nested package within @{code}xml@{body}, and @{code}minidom@{body} is a module within @{code}xml.dom@{body}.

That sounds complicated, but it's really not. Looking at the actual implementation may help. Packages are little more than directories of modules; nested packages are subdirectories. The modules within a package (or a nested package) are still just @{code}.py@{body} files, like always, except that they're in a subdirectory instead of the main @{code}lib/@{body} directory of your Python installation.

@{b}Example 9.6. File layout of a package@{ub}

@{code}
Python21/ root Python installation (home of the executable)
|
+--lib/ library directory (home of the standard library modules)
|
+-- xml/ xml package (really just a directory with other stuff in it)
|
+--sax/ xml.sax package (again, just a directory)
|
+--dom/ xml.dom package (contains minidom.py)
|
+--parsers/ xml.parsers package (used internally)
@{body}

So when you say @{code}from xml.dom import minidom@{body}, Python figures out that that means "look in the @{code}xml@{body} directory for a @{code}dom@{body} directory, and look in @{i}that@{ui} for the @{code}minidom@{body} module, and import it as @{code}minidom@{body} ". But Python is even smarter than that; not only can you import entire modules contained within a package, you can selectively import specific classes or functions from a module contained within a package. You can also import the package itself as a module. The syntax is all the same; Python figures out what you mean based on the file layout of the package, and automatically does the right thing.

@{b}Example 9.7. Packages are modules, too@{ub}

@{code}
>>> from xml.dom import minidom
>>> minidom

>>> minidom.Element

>>> from xml.dom.minidom import Element
>>> Element

>>> minidom.Element

>>> from xml import dom
>>> dom

>>> import xml
>>> xml

@{body}

Here you're importing a module (@{code}minidom@{body}) from a nested package (@{code}xml.dom@{body}). The result is that @{code}minidom@{body} is imported into your namespace, and in order to reference classes within the @{code}minidom@{body} module (like @{code}Element@{body}), you need to preface them with the module name.

Here you are importing a class (@{code}Element@{body}) from a module (@{code}minidom@{body}) from a nested package (@{code}xml.dom@{body}). The result is that @{code}Element@{body} is imported directly into your namespace. Note that this does not interfere with the previous import; the @{code}Element@{body} class can now be referenced in two ways (but it's all still the same class).

Here you are importing the @{code}dom@{body} package (a nested package of @{code}xml@{body}) as a module in and of itself. Any level of a package can be treated as a module, as you'll see in a moment. It can even have its own attributes and methods, just the modules you've seen before.

Here you are importing the root level @{code}xml@{body} package as a module.

So how can a package (which is just a directory on disk) be imported and treated as a module (which is always a file on disk)? The answer is the magical @{code}__init__.py@{body} file. You see, packages are not simply directories; they are directories with a specific file, @{code}__init__.py@{body}, inside. This file defines the attributes and methods of the package. For instance, @{code}xml.dom@{body} contains a @{code}Node@{body} class, which is defined in @{code}xml/dom/__init__.py@{body}. When you import a package as a module (like @{code}dom@{body} from @{code}xml@{body}), you're really importing its @{code}__init__.py@{body} file.

A package is a directory with the special @{code}__init__.py@{body} file in it. The @{code}__init__.py@{body} file defines the attributes and methods of the package. It doesn't need to define anything; it can just be an empty file, but it has to exist. But if @{code}__init__.py@{body} doesn't exist, the directory is just a directory, not a package, and it can't be imported or contain modules or nested packages.

So why bother with packages? Well, they provide a way to logically group related modules. Instead of having an @{code}xml@{body} package with @{code}sax@{body} and @{code}dom@{body} packages inside, the authors could have chosen to put all the @{code}sax@{body} functionality in @{code}xmlsax.py@{body} and all the @{code}dom@{body} functionality in @{code}xmldom.py@{body}, or even put all of it in a single module. But that would have been unwieldy (as of this writing, the XML package has over 3000 lines of code) and difficult to manage (separate source files mean multiple people can work on different areas simultaneously).

If you ever find yourself writing a large subsystem in Python (or, more likely, when you realize that your small subsystem has grown into a large one), invest some time designing a good package architecture. It's one of the many things Python is good at, so take advantage of it.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S3 "9.3. Parsing XML"
@TITLE "9.3. Parsing XML"
@NEXT "S4"
@PREV "S2"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.3. Parsing XML@{ub}

As I was saying, actually parsing an XML document is very simple: one line of code. Where you go from there is up to you.

@{b}Example 9.8. Loading an XML document (for real this time) @{ub}

@{code}

>>> from xml.dom import minidom
>>> xmldoc = minidom.parse('~/diveintopython/common/py/kgp/binary.xml')
>>> xmldoc

>>> print xmldoc.toxml()

0
1

\\

@{body}

As you saw in the previous section, this imports the @{code}minidom@{body} module from the @{code}xml.dom@{body} package.

Here is the one line of code that does all the work: @{code}minidom.parse@{body} takes one argument and returns a parsed representation of the XML document. The argument can be many things; in this case, it's simply a filename of an XML document on my local disk. (To follow along, you'll need to change the path to point to your downloaded examples directory.) But you can also pass a file object, or even a file-like object. You'll take advantage of this flexibility later in this chapter.

The object returned from @{code}minidom.parse@{body} is a @{code}Document@{body} object, a descendant of the @{code}Node@{body} class. This @{code}Document@{body} object is the root level of a complex tree-like structure of interlocking Python objects that completely represent the XML document you passed to @{code}minidom.parse@{body}.

@{code}toxml@{body} is a method of the @{code}Node@{body} class (and is therefore available on the @{code}Document@{body} object you got from @{code}minidom.parse@{body}). @{code}toxml@{body} prints out the XML that this @{code}Node@{body} represents. For the @{code}Document@{body} node, this prints out the entire XML document.

Now that you have an XML document in memory, you can start traversing through it.

@{b}Example 9.9. Getting child nodes@{ub}

@{code}

>>> xmldoc.childNodes
[]
>>> xmldoc.childNodes[0]

>>> xmldoc.firstChild

@{body}

Every @{code}Node@{body} has a @{code}childNodes@{body} attribute, which is a list of the @{code}Node@{body} objects. A @{code}Document@{body} always has only one child node, the root element of the XML document (in this case, the @{code}grammar@{body} element).

To get the first (and in this case, the only) child node, just use regular list syntax. Remember, there is nothing special going on here; this is just a regular Python list of regular Python objects.

Since getting the first child node of a node is a useful and common activity, the @{code}Node@{body} class has a @{code}firstChild@{body} attribute, which is synonymous with @{code}childNodes[0]@{body}. (There is also a @{code}lastChild@{body} attribute, which is synonymous with @{code}childNodes[-1]@{body}.)

@{b}Example 9.10. @{code}toxml@{body} works on any node @{ub}

@{code}

>>> grammarNode = xmldoc.firstChild
>>> print grammarNode.toxml()

0
1

\\

@{body}

Since the @{code}toxml@{body} method is defined in the @{code}Node@{body} class, it is available on any XML node, not just the @{code}Document@{body} element.

@{b}Example 9.11. Child nodes can be text@{ub}

@{code}

>>> grammarNode.childNodes
[, , \\
, , ]
>>> print grammarNode.firstChild.toxml()

>>> print grammarNode.childNodes[1].toxml()

0
1

>>> print grammarNode.childNodes[3].toxml()

\\

>>> print grammarNode.lastChild.toxml()

@{body}

Looking at the XML in @{code}binary.xml@{body}, you might think that the @{code}grammar@{body} has only two child nodes, the two @{code}ref@{body} elements. But you're missing something: the carriage returns! After the @{code}''@{body} and before the first @{code}''@{body} is a carriage return, and this text counts as a child node of the @{code}grammar@{body} element. Similarly, there is a carriage return after each @{code}''@{body}; these also count as child nodes. So @{code}grammar.childNodes@{body} is actually a list of 5 objects: 3 @{code}Text@{body} objects and 2 @{code}Element@{body} objects.

The first child is a @{code}Text@{body} object representing the carriage return after the @{code}''@{body} tag and before the first @{code}''@{body} tag.

The second child is an @{code}Element@{body} object representing the first @{code}ref@{body} element.

The fourth child is an @{code}Element@{body} object representing the second @{code}ref@{body} element.

The last child is a @{code}Text@{body} object representing the carriage return after the @{code}''@{body} end tag and before the @{code}''@{body} end tag.

@{b}Example 9.12. Drilling down all the way to text@{ub}

@{code}

>>> grammarNode

>>> refNode = grammarNode.childNodes[1]
>>> refNode

>>> refNode.childNodes
[, , , \\
, , \\
, ]
>>> pNode = refNode.childNodes[2]
>>> pNode

>>> print pNode.toxml()
0
>>> pNode.firstChild

>>> pNode.firstChild.data
u'0'
@{body}

As you saw in the previous example, the first @{code}ref@{body} element is @{code}grammarNode.childNodes[1]@{body}, since childNodes[0] is a @{code}Text@{body} node for the carriage return.

The @{code}ref@{body} element has its own set of child nodes, one for the carriage return, a separate one for the spaces, one for the @{code}p@{body} element, and so forth.

You can even use the @{code}toxml@{body} method here, deeply nested within the document.

The @{code}p@{body} element has only one child node (you can't tell that from this example, but look at @{code}pNode.childNodes@{body} if you don't believe me), and it is a @{code}Text@{body} node for the single character @{code}'0'@{body}.

The @{code}.data@{body} attribute of a @{code}Text@{body} node gives you the actual string that the text node represents. But what is that @{code}'u'@{body} in front of the string? The answer to that deserves its own section.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S4 "9.4. Unicode"
@TITLE "9.4. Unicode"
@NEXT "S5"
@PREV "S3"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.4. Unicode @{ub}

Unicode is a system to represent characters from all the world's different languages. When Python parses an XML document, all data is stored in memory as unicode.

You'll get to all that in a minute, but first, some background.

@{b}Historical note. @{ub} Before unicode, there were separate character encoding systems for each language, each using the same numbers (0-255) to represent that language's characters. Some languages (like Russian) have multiple conflicting standards about how to represent the same characters; other languages (like Japanese) have so many characters that they require multiple-byte character sets. Exchanging documents between systems was difficult because there was no way for a computer to tell for certain which character encoding scheme the document author had used; the computer only saw numbers, and the numbers could mean different things. Then think about trying to store these documents in the same place (like in the same database table); you would need to store the character encoding alongside each piece of text, and make sure to pass it around whenever you passed the text around. Then think about multilingual documents, with characters from multiple languages in the same document. (They typically used escape codes to switch modes; poof, you're in Russian koi8-r mode, so character 241 means this; poof, now you're in Mac Greek mode, so character 241 means something else. And so on.) These are the problems which unicode was designed to solve.

To solve these problems, unicode represents each character as a 2-byte number, from 0 to 65535.[5] Each 2-byte number represents a unique character used in at least one of the world's languages. (Characters that are used in multiple languages have the same numeric code.) There is exactly 1 number per character, and exactly 1 character per number. Unicode data is never ambiguous.

Of course, there is still the matter of all these legacy encoding systems. 7-bit ASCII, for instance, which stores English characters as numbers ranging from 0 to 127. (65 is capital "@{code}A@{body} ", 97 is lowercase "@{code}a@{body} ", and so forth.) English has a very simple alphabet, so it can be completely expressed in 7-bit ASCII. Western European languages like French, Spanish, and German all use an encoding system called ISO-8859-1 (also called "latin-1"), which uses the 7-bit ASCII characters for the numbers 0 through 127, but then extends into the 128-255 range for characters like n-with-a-tilde-over-it (241), and u-with-two-dots-over-it (252). And unicode uses the same characters as 7-bit ASCII for 0 through 127, and the same characters as ISO-8859-1 for 128 through 255, and then extends from there into characters for other languages with the remaining numbers, 256 through 65535.

When dealing with unicode data, you may at some point need to convert the data back into one of these other legacy encoding systems. For instance, to integrate with some other computer system which expects its data in a specific 1-byte encoding scheme, or to print it to a non-unicode-aware terminal or printer. Or to store it in an XML document which explicitly specifies the encoding scheme.

And on that note, let's get back to Python.

Python has had unicode support throughout the language since version 2.0. The XML package uses unicode to store all parsed XML data, but you can use unicode anywhere.

@{b}Example 9.13. Introducing unicode@{ub}

@{code}

>>> s = u'Dive in'
>>> s
u'Dive in'
>>> print s
Dive in
@{body}

To create a unicode string instead of a regular ASCII string, add the letter "@{code}u@{body} " before the string. Note that this particular string doesn't have any non-ASCII characters. That's fine; unicode is a superset of ASCII (a very large superset at that), so any regular ASCII string can also be stored as unicode.

When printing a string, Python will attempt to convert it to your default encoding, which is usually ASCII. (More on this in a minute.) Since this unicode string is made up of characters that are also ASCII characters, printing it has the same result as printing a normal ASCII string; the conversion is seamless, and if you didn't know that @{code}s@{body} was a unicode string, you'd never notice the difference.

@{b}Example 9.14. Storing non-ASCII characters @{ub}

@{code}

>>> s = u'La Pe\\xf1a'
>>> print s
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
UnicodeError: ASCII encoding error: ordinal not in range(128)
>>> print s.encode('latin-1')
La Peña
@{body}

The real advantage of unicode, of course, is its ability to store non-ASCII characters, like the Spanish "@{code}ñ@{body} " (@{code}n@{body} with a tilde over it). The unicode character code for the tilde-n is @{code}0xf1@{body} in hexadecimal (241 in decimal), which you can type like this: @{code}\\xf1@{body}.

Remember I said that the @{code}print@{body} function attempts to convert a unicode string to ASCII so it can print it? Well, that's not going to work here, because your unicode string contains non-ASCII characters, so Python raises a @{code}UnicodeError@{body} error.

Here's where the conversion-from-unicode-to-other-encoding-schemes comes in. @{code}s@{body} is a unicode string, but @{code}print@{body} can only print a regular string. To solve this problem, you call the @{code}encode@{body} method, available on every unicode string, to convert the unicode string to a regular string in the given encoding scheme, which you pass as a parameter. In this case, you're using @{code}latin-1@{body} (also known as @{code}iso-8859-1@{body}), which includes the tilde-n (whereas the default ASCII encoding scheme did not, since it only includes characters numbered 0 through 127).

Remember I said Python usually converted unicode to ASCII whenever it needed to make a regular string out of a unicode string? Well, this default encoding scheme is an option which you can customize.

@{b}Example 9.15. @{code}sitecustomize.py@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

# sitecustomize.py
# this file can be anywhere in your Python path,
# but it usually goes in ${pythondir}/lib/site-packages/
import sys
sys.setdefaultencoding('iso-8859-1')

@{body}

@{code}sitecustomize.py@{body} is a special script; Python will try to import it on startup, so any code in it will be run automatically. As the comment mentions, it can go anywhere (as long as @{code}import@{body} can find it), but it usually goes in the @{code}site-packages@{body} directory within your Python @{code}lib@{body} directory.

@{code}setdefaultencoding@{body} function sets, well, the default encoding. This is the encoding scheme that Python will try to use whenever it needs to auto-coerce a unicode string into a regular string.

@{b}Example 9.16. Effects of setting the default encoding@{ub}

@{code}

>>> import sys
>>> sys.getdefaultencoding()
'iso-8859-1'
>>> s = u'La Pe\\xf1a'
>>> print s
La Peña
@{body}

This example assumes that you have made the changes listed in the previous example to your @{code}sitecustomize.py@{body} file, and restarted Python. If your default encoding still says @{code}'ascii'@{body}, you didn't set up your @{code}sitecustomize.py@{body} properly, or you didn't restart Python. The default encoding can only be changed during Python startup; you can't change it later. (Due to some wacky programming tricks that I won't get into right now, you can't even call @{code}sys.setdefaultencoding@{body} after Python has started up. Dig into @{code}site.py@{body} and search for "@{code}setdefaultencoding@{body} " to find out how.)

Now that the default encoding scheme includes all the characters you use in your string, Python has no problem auto-coercing the string and printing it.

@{b}Example 9.17. Specifying encoding in @{code}.py@{body} files @{ub}

If you are going to be storing non-ASCII strings within your Python code, you'll need to specify the encoding of each individual @{code}.py@{body} file by putting an encoding declaration at the top of each file. This declaration defines the @{code}.py@{body} file to be UTF-8:

@{code}

#!/usr/bin/env python
# -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-

@{body}

Now, what about XML? Well, every XML document is in a specific encoding. Again, ISO-8859-1 is a popular encoding for data in Western European languages. KOI8-R is popular for Russian texts. The encoding, if specified, is in the header of the XML document.

@{b}Example 9.18. @{code}russiansample.xml@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

???????????

@{body}

This is a sample extract from a real Russian XML document; it's part of a Russian translation of this very book. Note the encoding, @{code}koi8-r@{body}, specified in the header.

These are Cyrillic characters which, as far as I know, spell the Russian word for "Preface". If you open this file in a regular text editor, the characters will most likely like gibberish, because they're encoded using the @{code}koi8-r@{body} encoding scheme, but they're being displayed in @{code}iso-8859-1@{body}.

@{b}Example 9.19. Parsing @{code}russiansample.xml@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

>>> from xml.dom import minidom
>>> xmldoc = minidom.parse('russiansample.xml')
>>> title = xmldoc.getElementsByTagName('title')[0].firstChild.data
>>> title
u'\\u041f\\u0440\\u0435\\u0434\\u0438\\u0441\\u043b\\u043e\\u0432\\u0438\\u0435'
>>> print title
Traceback (innermost last):
File "", line 1, in ?
UnicodeError: ASCII encoding error: ordinal not in range(128)
>>> convertedtitle = title.encode('koi8-r')
>>> convertedtitle
'\\xf0\\xd2\\xc5\\xc4\\xc9\\xd3\\xcc\\xcf\\xd7\\xc9\\xc5'
>>> print convertedtitle
???????????
@{body}

I'm assuming here that you saved the previous example as @{code}russiansample.xml@{body} in the current directory. I am also, for the sake of completeness, assuming that you've changed your default encoding back to @{code}'ascii'@{body} by removing your @{code}sitecustomize.py@{body} file, or at least commenting out the @{code}setdefaultencoding@{body} line.

Note that the text data of the @{code}title@{body} tag (now in the @{code}title@{body} variable, thanks to that long concatenation of Python functions which I hastily skipped over and, annoyingly, won't explain until the next section) -- the text data inside the XML document's @{code}title@{body} element is stored in unicode.

Printing the title is not possible, because this unicode string contains non-ASCII characters, so Python can't convert it to ASCII because that doesn't make sense.

You can, however, explicitly convert it to @{code}koi8-r@{body}, in which case you get a (regular, not unicode) string of single-byte characters (@{code}f0@{body}, @{code}d2@{body}, @{code}c5@{body}, and so forth) that are the @{code}koi8-r@{body} -encoded versions of the characters in the original unicode string.

Printing the @{code}koi8-r@{body} -encoded string will probably show gibberish on your screen, because your Python IDE is interpreting those characters as @{code}iso-8859-1@{body}, not @{code}koi8-r@{body}. But at least they do print. (And, if you look carefully, it's the same gibberish that you saw when you opened the original XML document in a non-unicode-aware text editor. Python converted it from @{code}koi8-r@{body} into unicode when it parsed the XML document, and you've just converted it back.)

To sum up, unicode itself is a bit intimidating if you've never seen it before, but unicode data is really very easy to handle in Python. If your XML documents are all 7-bit ASCII (like the examples in this chapter), you will literally never think about unicode. Python will convert the ASCII data in the XML documents into unicode while parsing, and auto-coerce it back to ASCII whenever necessary, and you'll never even notice. But if you need to deal with that in other languages, Python is ready.

@{b}Further reading@{ub}

* Unicode.org is the home page of the unicode standard, including a brief technical introduction.

* Unicode Tutorial has some more examples of how to use Python's unicode functions, including how to force Python to coerce unicode into ASCII even when it doesn't really want to.

* PEP 263 goes into more detail about how and when to define a character encoding in your @{code}.py@{body} files.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S5 "9.5. Searching for elements"
@TITLE "9.5. Searching for elements"
@NEXT "S6"
@PREV "S4"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.5. Searching for elements @{ub}

Traversing XML documents by stepping through each node can be tedious. If you're looking for something in particular, buried deep within your XML document, there is a shortcut you can use to find it quickly: @{code}getElementsByTagName@{body}.

For this section, you'll be using the @{code}binary.xml@{body} grammar file, which looks like this:

@{b}Example 9.20. @{code}binary.xml@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

0
1

\\

@{body}

It has two @{code}ref@{body} s, @{code}'bit'@{body} and @{code}'byte'@{body}. A @{code}bit@{body} is either a @{code}'0'@{body} or @{code}'1'@{body}, and a @{code}byte@{body} is 8 @{code}bit@{body} s.

@{b}Example 9.21. Introducing @{code}getElementsByTagName@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

>>> from xml.dom import minidom
>>> xmldoc = minidom.parse('binary.xml')
>>> reflist = xmldoc.getElementsByTagName('ref')
>>> reflist
[, ]
>>> print reflist[0].toxml()

0
1

>>> print reflist[1].toxml()

\\

@{body}

@{code}getElementsByTagName@{body} takes one argument, the name of the element you wish to find. It returns a list of @{code}Element@{body} objects, corresponding to the XML elements that have that name. In this case, you find two @{code}ref@{body} elements.

@{b}Example 9.22. Every element is searchable@{ub}

@{code}

>>> firstref = reflist[0]
>>> print firstref.toxml()

0
1

>>> plist = firstref.getElementsByTagName("p")
>>> plist
[, ]
>>> print plist[0].toxml()
0
>>> print plist[1].toxml()
1
@{body}

Continuing from the previous example, the first object in your @{code}reflist@{body} is the @{code}'bit'@{body} @{code}ref@{body} element.

You can use the same @{code}getElementsByTagName@{body} method on this @{code}Element@{body} to find all the @{code}@{body} elements within the @{code}'bit'@{body} @{code}ref@{body} element.

Just as before, the @{code}getElementsByTagName@{body} method returns a list of all the elements it found. In this case, you have two, one for each bit.

@{b}Example 9.23. Searching is actually recursive@{ub}

@{code}

>>> plist = xmldoc.getElementsByTagName("p")
>>> plist
[, , ]
>>> plist[0].toxml()
'0'
>>> plist[1].toxml()
'1'
>>> plist[2].toxml()
'\\
'
@{body}

Note carefully the difference between this and the previous example. Previously, you were searching for @{code}p@{body} elements within @{code}firstref@{body}, but here you are searching for @{code}p@{body} elements within @{code}xmldoc@{body}, the root-level object that represents the entire XML document. This @{i}does@{ui} find the @{code}p@{body} elements nested within the @{code}ref@{body} elements within the root @{code}grammar@{body} element.

The first two @{code}p@{body} elements are within the first @{code}ref@{body} (the @{code}'bit'@{body} @{code}ref@{body}).

The last @{code}p@{body} element is the one within the second @{code}ref@{body} (the @{code}'byte'@{body} @{code}ref@{body}).

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S6 "9.6. Accessing element attributes"
@TITLE "9.6. Accessing element attributes"
@NEXT "S7"
@PREV "S5"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.6. Accessing element attributes @{ub}

XML elements can have one or more attributes, and it is incredibly simple to access them once you have parsed an XML document.

For this section, you'll be using the @{code}binary.xml@{body} grammar file that you saw in the previous section.

This section may be a little confusing, because of some overlapping terminology. Elements in an XML document have attributes, and Python objects also have attributes. When you parse an XML document, you get a bunch of Python objects that represent all the pieces of the XML document, and some of these Python objects represent attributes of the XML elements. But the (Python) objects that represent the (XML) attributes also have (Python) attributes, which are used to access various parts of the (XML) attribute that the object represents. I told you it was confusing. I am open to suggestions on how to distinguish these more clearly.

@{b}Example 9.24. Accessing element attributes@{ub}

@{code}

>>> xmldoc = minidom.parse('binary.xml')
>>> reflist = xmldoc.getElementsByTagName('ref')
>>> bitref = reflist[0]
>>> print bitref.toxml()

0
1

>>> bitref.attributes

>>> bitref.attributes.keys()
[u'id']
>>> bitref.attributes.values()
[]
>>> bitref.attributes["id"]

@{body}

Each @{code}Element@{body} object has an attribute called @{code}attributes@{body}, which is a @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} object. This sounds scary, but it's not, because a @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} is an object that acts like a dictionary, so you already know how to use it.

Treating the @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} as a dictionary, you can get a list of the names of the attributes of this element by using @{code}attributes.keys()@{body}. This element has only one attribute, @{code}'id'@{body}.

Attribute names, like all other text in an XML document, are stored in unicode.

Again treating the @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} as a dictionary, you can get a list of the values of the attributes by using @{code}attributes.values()@{body}. The values are themselves objects, of type @{code}Attr@{body}. You'll see how to get useful information out of this object in the next example.

Still treating the @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} as a dictionary, you can access an individual attribute by name, using normal dictionary syntax. (Readers who have been paying extra-close attention will already know how the @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} class accomplishes this neat trick: by defining a @{code}__getitem__@{body} special method. Other readers can take comfort in the fact that they don't need to understand how it works in order to use it effectively.)

@{b}Example 9.25. Accessing individual attributes@{ub}

@{code}

>>> a = bitref.attributes["id"]
>>> a

>>> a.name
u'id'
>>> a.value
u'bit'
@{body}

The @{code}Attr@{body} object completely represents a single XML attribute of a single XML element. The name of the attribute (the same name as you used to find this object in the @{code}bitref.attributes@{body} @{code}NamedNodeMap@{body} pseudo-dictionary) is stored in @{code}a.name@{body}.

The actual text value of this XML attribute is stored in @{code}a.value@{body}.

Like a dictionary, attributes of an XML element have no ordering. Attributes may @{i}happen to be@{ui} listed in a certain order in the original XML document, and the @{code}Attr@{body} objects may @{i}happen to be@{ui} listed in a certain order when the XML document is parsed into Python objects, but these orders are arbitrary and should carry no special meaning. You should always access individual attributes by name, like the keys of a dictionary.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S7 "9.7. Segue"
@TITLE "9.7. Segue"
@NEXT "MAIN"
@PREV "S6"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}9.7. Segue @{ub}

OK, that's it for the hard-core XML stuff. The next chapter will continue to use these same example programs, but focus on other aspects that make the program more flexible: using streams for input processing, using @{code}getattr@{body} for method dispatching, and using command-line flags to allow users to reconfigure the program without changing the code.

Before moving on to the next chapter, you should be comfortable doing all of these things:

* Parsing XML documents using @{code}minidom@{body}, searching through the parsed document, and accessing arbitrary element attributes and element children

* Organizing complex libraries into packages

* Converting unicode strings to different character encodings

@{line}

[5] This, sadly, is @{i}still@{ui} an oversimplification. Unicode now has been extended to handle ancient Chinese, Korean, and Japanese texts, which had so many different characters that the 2-byte unicode system could not represent them all. But Python doesn't currently support that out of the box, and I don't know if there is a project afoot to add it. You've reached the limits of my expertise, sorry.

@{b}<a

